+++
title: Post-imperial Assyria
description: Fifth period of Assyrian history
key-facts:
  - name: Where the period starts
    value: 609 BC, with the fall of the Neo-Assyrian Empire
  - name: Under the Persians
    value: Conquered by the Achaemenid Empire in 539 BC
  - name: Under the Greeks
    value: Taken in the conquests of Alexander the Great, 330 BC
  - name: Under the Parthians
    value: Conquered by the Parthian Empire c. 141 BC, and flourishing under
      Parthian suzerainty in the 1st–2nd centuries AD
  - name: Where the period ends
    value: Sack of Assur by the Sasanian Empire, c. AD 240
  - name: Languages in use
    value: Aramaic, Akkadian, Greek
image-plan:
  - file: Detail._Parthian_stele_from_Ashur,_Iraq._1st_century_BCE_to_1st_century_CE._Museum_of_Archaeology,_Istanbul,_Turkey.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: Detail of a stele erected at Assur in the 2nd century AD, under
      Parthian rule, by the local ruler Rʻuth-Assor. It is in the style of the
      Neo-Assyrian royal steles, centuries after the last Assyrian king.
  - file: Nabonidus.jpg
    role: inline
    section: neo-babylonian-rule
    caption: Stele of Nabonidus (r. 556–539 BC), the last Neo-Babylonian king,
      found at Harran — the city whose great temple to the lunar god Sîn was
      rebuilt in his reign.
  - file: Achaemenid_(Persian)_Empire_-_Circa_480BC.png
    role: full
    section: achaemenid-rule
    caption: The Achaemenid Empire under Xerxes I, c. 480 BC. Most of Assyria was
      organized as the single province Athura, which kept the region on the
      map as a distinct political entity.
  - file: Xerxes_I_tomb_Assyrian_soldier_circa_470_BCE.jpg
    role: inline
    section: achaemenid-rule
    caption: An Assyrian soldier depicted on the tomb of Xerxes I at Naqsh-e
      Rostam. Some Achaemenid tombs show the Assyrians as one of the empire's
      ethnic groups, alongside the others.
  - file: Partition_of_Babylon_323_BC.png
    role: full
    section: seleucid-rule
    caption: The satrapies allotted at the Partition of Babylon in 323 BC, after
      the death of Alexander the Great. The majority of these lands eventually
      came under the rule of the Seleucid Empire.
  - file: Northern_Mesopotamian_vassal_kingdoms_AD_200.png
    role: full
    section: organization-and-revival
    caption: "Northern Mesopotamia in AD 200: the semi-autonomous kingdoms of
      Osroene, Adiabene and Hatra, and the semi-autonomous Assur city-state.
      Osroene was a Roman vassal, the others Parthian. Sites and cities of the
      former Assyrian heartland occupied at this time are also marked."
  - file: Hatra-109726.jpg
    role: inline
    section: parthian-assur
    caption: The Great Iwans at Hatra. The Ashur temple rebuilt at Assur was
      likely similar to this building in appearance and size.
  - file: Parthian_king_ashur.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeological-evidence
    caption: Terracotta figure of a Parthian king, found at Assur, where the most
      extensive finds of the whole post-imperial period come from.
  - file: Parthian_jewelry_from_Nineveh_by_Nickmard_Khoey.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeological-evidence
    caption: Parthian jewelry found at Nineveh, resettled under the Seleucids and
      Parthians after centuries of desertion.
  - file: Statue_of_Hermes,_from_Nineveh,_Iraq._2nd_century_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: religion
    caption: A 2nd-century BC statue of the Greek god Hermes, found at Nineveh.
      The deities worshipped there were nearly all syncretistic or outright
      imported figures.
further:
  - slug: neo-assyrian-empire
    why: The empire whose fall in 609 BC opens this period, and the yardstick
      everything after it is measured against — the settlement density of
      Parthian Assyria is only comparable to what the region was like under
      it.
  - slug: babylon
    why: "Where the cult statue of Ashur went after Assur was sacked in 614: kept
      in the Esagila temple until Cyrus returned it."
  - slug: history-of-the-assyrians
    why: The Assyrian people survived the end of their civilization and remain an
      ethnic minority in the region and elsewhere to this day.
